 #:seon.task{:name "design-a-pathless-reference-section"
             :plan "## Problem\n\n`seon.ref/section` of a reference with no path, such as `\"#the-goal\"`, throws instead of answering a missing-file `:seon.ref/section-error`, as it does for a path that names no file. On main:\n\n```clojure\n(seon.ref/section \"#the-goal\")\n;; throws: seon.ref/locate refused argument 0 `path`: expected :seon.ref/path (a string of length at least 1), got a string.\n```\n"
             :instructions "## Work\n\nMake the intent say exactly what the problem is, in terms of the data. Read `## Problem` first. Then search the system for what already exists: `my.program/search` with the words of the problem, `docs/seon/architecture/vocabulary.md` for the words we use, `my.program/source` on each owner you find. Change the plan with `my.task/update!`, passing the whole new plan text: `(my.task/update! the-subject :seon.task/plan (constantly whole-new-plan))`. Keep `## Problem` as it is and add:\n\n- `## What exists`: the producer of the wrong or missing value, its callers, the schemas its inputs and outputs use, and the tests that cover it, each cited in backticks as a fully qualified symbol or keyword.\n- `## Approach`: the data first (the schema keys involved, and any schema to add or change, registered on this branch), then each function to change or add with its name, arity and complete contract over named schemas, and what it replaces or deletes. A new function is a `defn` in its owner's namespace with its full contract and the body `(throw (ex-info \"not yet\" {:seon.error/type :seon.error/unimplemented}))`.\n\nBacktick every name you cite: a backticked name that does not resolve shows up as a problem to fix.\n\n## Done\n\nThe three sections have content, every reference resolves, the intent names the schemas involved, and every function you defined or changed has a complete contract. Your proofs show what is missing after each evaluation.\n"}
